Plain CSS lines: 300+

Plain CSS build time: 60 minutes

Part B — Dynamic Class Bug

I created a component where the background color comes from a variable.

For example:
const color = "blue";
const className = `bg-${color}-500`; 

This creates: bg-blue-500

Since bg-${color}-500 is created dynamically, Tailwind may not detect bg-blue-500

to fix Use complete class names

I can create a list of complete classes:

const colors = {
    blue: "bg-blue-500",
    red: "bg-red-500",
    green: "bg-green-500"
};


Part C — Refactor for Reuse

The pricing card was repeated three times for Basic, Pro, and Business. Since the structure of all three cards is almost the same, I can create one reusable component.

React component
function PricingCard({ name, price }) {
    return (
        <div className="card">
            <h2>{name}</h2>
            <p>${price}/month</p>
            <button>Choose Plan</button>
        </div>
    );
}

Then I can use it like this:

<PricingCard name="Basic" price="9" />
<PricingCard name="Pro" price="19" />
<PricingCard name="Business" price="39" />

The output will still show the three pricing cards, but I only have to write the card structure once.

Using @apply

I can also use Tailwind's @apply to combine common utility classes:

.card {
    @apply flex flex-col rounded-xl bg-white p-8;
}

Then I can use:

<div class="card">
    <h2>Basic</h2>
    <p>$9/month</p>
    <button>Choose Plan</button>
</div>
Comparison — 200 words

The reusable component and @apply both help reduce repeated code, but they solve different problems. A reusable component creates one common structure for the pricing card. The name, price, features, and other information can be passed to the component as values. This means I can create many pricing cards without copying the complete HTML each time. If I need to change the card structure later, I only need to change it in one place.

@apply is mainly useful for grouping repeated Tailwind styling. For example, I can create a .card class containing common Tailwind utilities such as padding, border radius, background color, and flexbox properties. This makes the HTML shorter, but the card HTML still needs to be repeated.

For a small static page, @apply can be simple and useful. However, for a real application, I would choose the reusable component approach. Components are easier to reuse when the application becomes larger and the cards need different data or behavior. I would still use Tailwind classes for styling and use @apply only when it makes sense for repeated styles. For a product that will be developed for two years, reusable components would be my main choice because they make future changes easier.


# Part E — Tailwind Discussion

A senior developer might say that **Tailwind is just inline CSS with extra steps**. Their main point is that we write many classes directly inside the HTML. For example, a button might have classes like `bg-blue-500`, `p-3`, `rounded-lg`, and `text-white`. This can make the HTML look long and difficult to read. With normal CSS, we can give an element a simple class name like `.button` and keep all the styling in a CSS file.

I understand this argument, but I don't completely agree. Tailwind is different from inline styles because its classes follow a **fixed and consistent design system**. It already provides common spacing, colours, font sizes, responsive styles, hover effects, and dark mode. This means developers do not have to create a new CSS rule for every small change. Tailwind also helps keep the design consistent across the whole project.

For a **5-person team building a product for 2 years**, I would choose Tailwind. A small team can work faster because everyone can use the same set of classes and design rules. It also reduces the amount of custom CSS that the team needs to maintain.

However, I would not use Tailwind for everything. For repeated or complicated parts of the website, I would create **reusable components**. I would also use normal CSS when something is difficult or messy to create with Tailwind.

**My final decision is to use Tailwind with reusable components.** I think this gives a good balance between fast development, consistent design, and easy maintenance for a long-term project.
